      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I also want a lengthable variable\nSince 0 to 1 is often good for animation control and there is no need to float, 4bit fixed (0 to 1) can reduce the number of bits.\nSpecifically, I want 40 values to control each joint of the finger, but if it is 256 bits, only 31 values can be used (because Emote is forced to bring 1 value). If 4bit fixed is implemented, it will be 62 values, which is sufficient.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-02-21T21:58:00.947Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I don't know if this is a limitation in Unity, but I would love to see different sized ints as an option for advanced avatar creation.\nA use-case example: avatar skin textures. A lot of avatars will use a float or int to cycle the tone from a limited list of tones, like say 0 = pale, 1 = light, 2 = tan, 3 = dark. With a 2-bit int that could be accomplished while saving 6 bits for other parameters. \nOther use cases could include something like an avatar with 7 dancing emotes that would only need 3 bits. 8-bit is just too much most of the time.",
